	In problems 4#, invert the matrix.
	
	#. $$[[4, 1 ; -1, 2]].$$
	
	#. $$[[1, 0, -1 ; 4, 5, 6 ; 0, -1, 2]].$$
	
	#. $$[[2, 1, 3 ; 3, -1, 2 ; 1, 0, 1]].$$
	
	#. $$[[4]].$$
	

	- - -

	#. Let $$A = [[a, b ; c, d]]$$ be a generic $2 \times 2$ matrix. Use **row reduction** (not a 2x2 inverse formula) to find a formula for $A^{-1}$ in terms of $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$. What condition does this enforce on $a$, $b$, $c$, and $d$?



	- - -

	Problems 3# concern the following situation. Suppose that one year from now, the economy will be in one of three states: a boom, a normal year, or a bust. You can buy three kinds of assets right now, each of which pays a different amount in each situation next year:

	> - A **bond** costs $\$95$ today and pays $\$100$ next year no matter what.

	> - A share of **stock** costs $\$180$ today and pays $\$400$ in a boom, $\$200$ in a normal year, and nothing in a bust.

	> - An **insurance contract** costs $\$125$ today and pays nothing in a boom, $\$100$ in a normal year, and $\$300$ in a bust.

	You can buy any amount of each asset, including fractions. You can also **short sell**, which means holding a negative amount: you receive the price today and owe the payoff next year. For example, short selling a bond is the same as borrowing $\$95$ today and paying back $\$100$ next year.

	#. Suppose you buy $b$ bonds, $s$ stocks, and $c$ insurance contracts, and

	$$
		\vec{x} = [[ b ; s ; c ]]
	$$

	is a vector representing your portfolio. Find a matrix $A$ so that $A\vec{x}$ is a vector in $#R#^3$ whose components are your total payouts (i.e. revenue, not profit) next year in each of the three scenarios.

	#. A bank wants to sell a new asset that pays $\$300$ in a boom, $\$100$ in a normal year, and nothing in a bust. Find the portfolio $\vec{x}$ that pays exactly the same as this new asset in every scenario; this is called a **replicating portfolio**. How much does the replicating portfolio cost today?

	#. Suppose the bank doesn't do this work and prices the new asset at $\$100$ instead. Describe a set of trades that gives you money today and leaves you owing nothing next year, no matter which state happens (called an **arbitrage**). What do you expect will happen to the price of the new asset as people take advantage of it?

